import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

## The jobs package: what it is for, installing the plugin, a job, queueing one, schedules,
## attempts, time limits and keys, how the workers run jobs, reading the queue, the jobs page, and
## what it doesn't do yet.
meta = {
	title: "Jobs and cron",
	description: "packages/jobs, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: jobs queued in the app's database (Postgres or SQLite) and run by workers until they succeed, schedules, and a page of the queue.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "jobs", label: "A job" },
		{ id: "queue", label: "Queueing one" },
		{ id: "cron", label: "On a schedule" },
		{ id: "options", label: "Attempts, time limits and keys" },
		{ id: "workers", label: "How jobs run" },
		{ id: "listing", label: "Reading the queue" },
		{ id: "page", label: "The jobs page" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · jobs and cron"
				title="Work that runs later, and again until it is done"
			>
				Some work shouldn't hold up a request: sending a welcome mail, a nightly cleanup, a call to a slow
				service. A job is that work, queued as a row in your app's database (Postgres or SQLite) and run in
				the background by workers. If it fails, it runs again, a little later each time. A schedule queues a
				job when its time comes. Email is best sent from a job
				(<a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>packages/email</a>), so a mail server that doesn't answer is tried
				again.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Jobs and cron are a package, <code>packages/jobs</code>, and a
				<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem}#plugins"}>plugin</a>: a package that adds to the server. It is Roc,
				built on what the server platform gives any plugin: the database and its notifications, background
				loops, and calls that run in isolation (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in your app's header, and list its plugin in <code>App.program</code>. This app
				lists the jobs page too, from <a href={Paths.ecosystem_admin}>packages/admin</a>:
			</p>
			<Code source={"app [program] { admin: \"…/packages/admin/main.roc\", jobs: \"…/packages/jobs/main.roc\", pf: platform \"…/server/platform/main.roc\" }"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.jobs_plugin.file}, in part"} source={Sources.jobs_plugin.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Job.plugin</code> takes one record. Only <code>jobs</code> is required:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>jobs</code></td><td>(required)</td><td>the kinds of job the workers run: <code>App.jobs</code>, every kind your app's modules define, which <code>rocx build</code> finds</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>crons</code></td><td>none</td><td>the schedules, each a <code>Cron.every(…)</code> (<a href="#cron">below</a>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>workers</code></td><td>4</td><td>how many jobs run at once</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>retry_ms</code></td><td>1000</td><td>how long a failed job waits before its second attempt; doubled for each attempt after</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>on_failed!</code></td><td>nothing more</td><td>what to do with a job that failed for good, after it is logged: given its kind, id, attempts and error (for an error tracker, <a href={Paths.ecosystem_posthog}>packages/posthog</a>'s <code>PostHog.job_failed!</code>)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The plugin adds the queue's table, <code>rocx_jobs</code> (its migration is recorded as
				<code>jobs/0001_jobs</code>), the workers and the schedules' loop. When the server starts, it checks
				every schedule, queues again the jobs a stop cut short, and deletes done jobs older than a week.
				Without the plugin, queueing a job is an error that says to list it. Without a database, the plugin
				runs nothing, and queueing a job returns the database's error.
			</p>

			<Heading id="jobs">A job</Heading>
			<p>
				A kind of job has a name and a function that does the work. The function is given your app's
				context (what its <code>init!</code> made) and the job's input, which is any type Roc can write as
				JSON. It answers <code>Ok({"{}"})</code> when the work is done, or <code>Err(why)</code> when it
				isn't, and then the job runs again later.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Define kinds at the top of a module's block, as here: <code>rocx build</code> finds every
				<code>Job.define</code> and <code>Job.define_with</code> there and lists them in <code>App.jobs</code>.
				In a program written by hand, list <code>Job.handler(kind)</code> for each kind instead.
			</p>

			<Heading id="queue">Queueing one</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_enqueue.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_enqueue.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Job.enqueue!(kind, input, {})"}</code> queues a job to run as soon as a worker is free.
				<code>{"{ when: At(ms) }"}</code> queues it not before that moment, in milliseconds since the epoch, as
				<code>Time.now_millis!</code> tells it (a moment in the past means now); <code>when</code> is
				<code>Now</code> unless a call says. The input is written as JSON, and read back as the kind's input
				type when the job runs. The options are a record whose fields have defaults, so a call says only what
				it changes, and more can be added later without changing any call.
			</p>
			<p>
				Queued inside <code>Db.transaction!</code>, a job is a row in the transaction: it runs only if the
				transaction commits (<a href={"${Paths.platform_database}#transactions"}>transactions</a>).
				<code>Job</code>'s errors are open unions (<code>[JobErr(Str), ..others]</code>), as <code>Db</code>'s
				are. So one transaction's body can use <code>?</code> on its statements, its jobs and your app's own
				errors together, and its caller matches <code>JobErr</code> beside <code>DbErr</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="cron">On a schedule</Heading>
			<Code source={"Cron.every(\"0 3 * * *\", nightly, {})       # five fields, UTC: at 03:00 each day\nCron.every(\"* * * * * *\", tick_job, {})    # six begin with seconds: every second"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A schedule, in the plugin's <code>crons</code>, queues its job with the given input each time it comes
				round. It uses cron's syntax: five fields (<code>minute hour day month weekday</code>), or six with
				seconds first, always in UTC. A field is <code>*</code>, a number, a range (<code>1-5</code>), a step
				(<code>*/15</code>) or a list of them (<code>1,15,30</code>); months and weekdays may be named
				(<code>JAN</code>, <code>MON</code>).
			</p>
			<p>
				Each job a schedule queues is keyed by the schedule and the second it was due, so a restart never
				queues it twice. Since it is a job, a run that fails is tried again like any other. The parser is Roc
				too: a schedule it can't read, or one whose job the plugin doesn't run, stops the server at start,
				saying why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="options">Attempts, time limits and keys</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job_options.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job_options.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Attempts and a time limit.</strong> <code>{"Job.define_with(name, { attempts, timeout_ms }, run!)"}</code>
					makes a kind that is tried <code>attempts</code> times in all, each run within
					<code>timeout_ms</code> milliseconds (0 means no limit). A kind made with <code>Job.define</code> is
					tried five times, with no limit.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Keys.</strong> <code>{"Job.enqueue_keyed!(kind, input, { key })"}</code> (and
					<code>when</code>, as for <code>enqueue!</code>) answers
					<code>Queued</code>, or <code>Exists</code> when a job with that key is already queued or running, and
					then queues nothing. Once that job is done, failed or canceled, the key is free again. A key names
					what the job is for (<code>reminder:ada</code>), so you can find the job by it:
					<code>Job.cancel!(key)</code> cancels the queued ones (a running one isn't stopped), and
					<code>{"Job.move!(key, when)"}</code> moves the queued one to another time. Each says how many jobs it
					changed, and inside a transaction takes effect only if the transaction commits.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="workers">How jobs run</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Each job is taken once.</strong> The workers are background loops. Each takes the next job
					that is due, with <code>for update skip locked</code> on Postgres, so no two workers take the same
					job. It runs the job in a call of its own, so a crash in a job is that job's failure, not the
					worker's. A worker waiting for work pauses without holding a thread.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Woken, not polling.</strong> Queueing a job notifies <code>rocx_jobs</code> (Postgres's
					<code>NOTIFY</code>, or <code>Db.notify!</code> on SQLite) when its transaction commits, and that
					wakes the workers. Otherwise a worker sleeps until the next delayed job or retry is due.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Again, and later.</strong> A job that fails (an <code>Err</code>, a crash, or its time limit
					passing) runs again after <code>retry_ms</code>, then twice that, and so on, until its kind's attempts
					are used up. Then it is <code>failed</code>, with its last error, and stays in
					<code>rocx_jobs</code> until you retry it. Each failure is logged as a warning, and a job that failed
					for good as an error, before <code>on_failed!</code> is called.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Within a time limit.</strong> A kind's <code>timeout_ms</code> is a deadline on each run.
					The first effect that waits past it (a database statement, a socket, a request to another server)
					ends the run, as a crash would, whatever the job does with errors. The statement is cancelled (on
					SQLite, interrupted) and its connection closed, what the run allocated is freed, and the job fails
					with <code>timed out after …</code>. Roc that never waits, such as a loop with no effect in it, is
					stopped too: the server interrupts its thread.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Across restarts.</strong> A job that was running when the server stopped runs again at the
					next start.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Another plugin's jobs.</strong> A package can bring its own kind of job, as
					<a href={Paths.ecosystem_push}>packages/push</a> does for sending. Its plugin lists
					<code>Job.call(kind)</code> in its calls and <code>Job.adopt!(kind)</code> in its starts, and the
					workers run those jobs too.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="listing">Reading the queue</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job_list.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job_list.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Job.list!({ state, kind, before_id, limit })"}</code> reads jobs, newest first; <code>""</code>
				and <code>0</code> mean any. Each is a <code>Job.Row</code>: its id, kind, input (as JSON), state
				(<code>queued</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>failed</code> or
				<code>canceled</code>), attempts and <code>max_attempts</code>, key, last error, and its times in
				milliseconds. The rest of what the jobs page is made of is there for your own pages too:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>Job.get!(id)</code> reads one job, and <code>{"Job.counts!({})"}</code> counts the jobs of each kind in each state.</li>
				<li><code>Job.retry!(id)</code> queues a failed or canceled job again, <code>Job.cancel_id!(id)</code> cancels a queued one, and <code>Job.run_now!(id)</code> makes a queued one due now.</li>
				<li><code>{"Cron.schedules!({})"}</code> lists the schedules with when each comes round next, and <code>Cron.run_now!(name)</code> queues a schedule's job now.</li>
				<li><code>{"Job.kinds!({})"}</code> lists the kinds the plugin runs, and <code>Job.changes</code> is the live topic on which the workers say what they start and finish.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="page">The jobs page</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_jobs_page.file}, in part"} source={"${Sources.platform_admins_only.rocx}\n\n# … in its plugins\n${Sources.platform_jobs_page.rocx}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_admin}>packages/admin</a> draws a page of the queue, as a plugin you list where
				only your admins get in. <code>{"JobsPage.plugin({ at, guard, back })"}</code> answers at
				<code>at</code> (<code>/admin/jobs</code> by default) behind your app's middleware, then
				<code>guard</code>: the middleware that knows who is an admin. <code>guard</code> has no default, so a
				jobs page is never public because an option was left out; without it the app doesn't compile. The page
				needs the jobs plugin. <code>back</code> is a link back to your app's own pages, first in the page's
				sidebar (none by default).
			</p>
			<p>
				To mount the page yourself, answer a route with <code>{"JobsPage.respond!(request, { mount })"}</code>
				from a <code>respond!</code>, which <a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#routes"}>answers every method</a>:
			</p>
			<Code title="routes/admin/jobs/+server.roc" source={"import admin.JobsPage\nimport ../../../Middlewares\n\nmiddleware = Middlewares.admin\n\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| JobsPage.respond!(request, { mount: \"/admin/jobs\" })"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>What it shows.</strong> A console of the queue: how many jobs are queued, running, failed,
					done and canceled; what finished in each five minutes of the last hour (done, done after a retry,
					failed); the latest failures, with why; each kind, with its attempts, its time limit and its jobs in
					each state; the schedules, in words where it can (<code>0 3 * * *</code> is daily at 03:00), with
					when each comes round next; and the jobs, fifty a page, newest first, each with its state, attempts,
					times, key and last error. The sidebar narrows the list to a state or a kind.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One job</strong> (<code>?id=…</code>) opens in a drawer over the list: its input (the JSON,
					laid out), its last error, its attempts, time limit and key, and its timeline from queued to
					finished.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What it does.</strong> Retry, cancel and run now (for a job, or for a schedule) are plain
					forms that post to the page and redirect back to where they came from. An action that can't be done
					is a page that says why (409), and a post from another site's page is refused (403).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>As jobs change</strong>, it refreshes itself over a stream of events
					(<code>?events</code>), and its times count on (<code>3 s ago</code>); Escape closes the drawer.
					Without script it is the same page, with a button that refreshes it. It follows the reader's light
					or dark scheme and fits a phone. It needs no components and no hydration, so it works whatever your
					pages are made of.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A job's log</strong> isn't shown on the jobs page: what a run logged is in the server's log,
					with its job's id. The options are in <code>packages/jobs/PROPOSALS.md</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Missed schedules</strong> aren't caught up: a time a schedule came round while the server was
					stopped is skipped, and the schedule goes on from the next one.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The server's tests queue jobs now and later, and in transactions that commit and that roll back. They
					fail jobs a few times and for good, crash them, and cut them off at their time limit (on Postgres, on
					SQLite, on a socket, on another server, and in Roc that loops forever without waiting), checking that
					nothing is left held. They queue, cancel and move jobs by key, list them, use the jobs page's forms
					behind a guard, leave alone a job another app queued, and run a schedule every second.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
